    "narrative": "Our offensive floor is bolstered today by **James Wood** (76.28) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.98), who headline our strongest plays. **James Wood** earns his elite status by leading off against J.T. Ginn, supported by an elite .437 xwOBA and a substantial park/order boost. Similarly, **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a massive 14% platoon advantage against Lowder and a high-leverage spot in the #2 hole. With **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (72.72) also providing high-efficiency output against favorable matchups, we are well-positioned to capitalize on volume, provided the pending lineups for **James Wood** and **Tyler Soderstrom** hold firm in their projected prime slots.\n\nThe benching of **Austin Riley** (52.17) remains the most difficult decision of the day; while his profile is solid, he falls victim to a crowded field of higher-upside plays. He narrowly missed the cut due to the superior matchup efficiency of players like **Isaac Paredes**, who faces a softer arm in Triston Rogers. Meanwhile, we continue to monitor **Zach McKinstry** (49.62); while his SP skill boost is notable against Grayson Rodriguez, he remains a marginal start strictly due to the lack of official lineup confirmation. We are also avoiding **Travis Bazzana** (36.05) entirely as he falls well below our 40.0 efficiency floor against the tough arm of Montgomery, and we remain vigilant regarding the rainout status of **Jose Caballero**. Owners should note that while **Corbin Carroll** (69.43) remains a start, his aggressive baserunning upside is slightly tempered by a matchup against May, though his elite speed remains our primary weapon in the scarcity-driven SB category.",
